        "**Who was Saloth Sar?** He was born in **1925** (some sources say 1928) into a fairly comfortable farming family in Kampong Thom province. Relatives worked at the royal palace, which helped him get places at good schools. In **1949** he won a scholarship to study radio-electronics in **Paris**. There he failed his exams, lost his scholarship — and found **communism**, joining the French Communist Party and a circle of Cambodian students who admired Stalin and, later, Mao.\n\n**Teacher and secret revolutionary.** Back in Cambodia from 1953, he taught French and history at a private school in Phnom Penh. Former pupils remembered him as calm and softly spoken. In secret he rose through the underground communist movement and by **1963** led the party. That year, fearing Sihanouk's police, he fled to the jungle. Later, living among hill peoples in the remote north-east, who had little use for money or markets, strengthened his belief that a simple rural society was 'pure'.\n\n**A secretive party.** The party was renamed the **Communist Party of Kampuchea** in 1966. Leaders used code names; members were told almost nothing; outsiders knew it only by Sihanouk's nickname, the **Khmer Rouge**.\n\n**Ideas.** Khmer Rouge thinking was an extreme form of **agrarian communism**:\n- Poor peasants were the 'pure' heart of the nation; **cities** were corrupt and foreign.\n- Cambodia must be totally **self-reliant**, needing no other country.\n- **Foreign influence** — especially Vietnam — and the **educated** classes were seen as enemies.\n- Everyone must serve **Angkar** ('the Organisation'), the faceless ruler that demanded total loyalty.\n\n**Recruiting the young.** The movement recruited heavily among **poor, rural teenagers**, some very young. With little education and often bereaved by war, they could be trained in total obedience.\n\n**Victory: 17 April 1975.** By early 1975 Phnom Penh was surrounded and cut off. Lon Nol fled on 1 April, and on **17 April 1975** Khmer Rouge fighters marched into the city. Many exhausted residents cheered, hoping for peace. Within hours, they were ordered to leave.\n\n| Factor | Evidence |\n|---|---|\n| Crisis | Bombing, invasion, refugees, civil war |\n| Weak government | Lon Nol's corruption and dependence on US aid |\n| Leadership | Secret, disciplined party; Sihanouk's borrowed charisma |",
