      discovery: {
        problem:
          "**Prediction.** Imagine a government that wants every person to be an equal rice farmer, loyal only to the state — with no ties to money, God, teachers, the outside world or even their own family.\n\nList **five things** you predict it would abolish or ban. Then think: what would ordinary life feel like on the first day?",
        idea:
          "The Khmer Rouge abolished almost everything on most people's lists: **money, markets, private property, schools, religion, the postal service, contact with the outside world** — and they broke up **family life**, sending children to work groups and making many families eat in communal kitchens. Their aim was to wipe out the past and restart history. A French priest who witnessed the beginning, François Ponchaud, called his 1977 book *Cambodia: Year Zero*, and the phrase stuck.",
      },
      body:
        "**Emptying the cities.** Within days of **17 April 1975**, the Khmer Rouge forced the whole population of **Phnom Penh** — swollen by refugees to perhaps **2 million** people — to leave. People were told it was for 'three days' because American planes would bomb the city. Hospitals were emptied; the old, sick and very young walked for days under the hot sun, and many died on the roads. Other towns were emptied too. Most families never saw their homes again.\n\n**Year Zero.** The new state, named **Democratic Kampuchea** from 1976, tried to destroy the old society completely:\n- **Money and markets** were abolished — the Khmer Rouge even blew up the National Bank building.\n- **Private property** was banned; everything belonged to Angkar.\n- **Schools and universities** were closed; books were destroyed.\n- **Religion** was banned: Buddhist monks were forced out of the monasteries, and Islam and Christianity were suppressed.\n- **Family life** was broken up: children were taken into work units and taught that their true parent was Angkar. Marriages were arranged by the regime.\n\n**Forced labour.** Almost everyone was sent to rural **collectives** to dig canals and dams and grow rice for 12 hours a day or more. A **Four-Year Plan** (1976) demanded about **3 tonnes of rice per hectare** — roughly three times what Cambodian farms usually produced. When the impossible quotas failed, local leaders blamed hidden 'enemies' rather than the plan. Rations shrank and **famine** spread.\n\n**'New people' and 'base people'.**\n\n| 'New people' | 'Base people' |\n|---|---|\n| Evacuated from towns after 17 April 1975 | Villagers already in Khmer Rouge areas before 1975 |\n| Seen as tainted by city life and foreign ideas | Seen as more loyal and 'pure' |\n| Lowest rations, harshest work, most suspicion | Somewhat better treatment — though many also suffered |\n\n**Isolation.** Borders were sealed, almost all foreign embassies were closed and there was no post, telephone or foreign news. Cambodia became one of the most isolated countries on Earth, which also meant the outside world knew little of what was happening inside.",
      diagram: `<svg viewBox="0 0 480 300" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Comparison chart: life before 1975 compared with Democratic Kampuchea 1975 to 1979. Money and markets abolished; schools closed; religion banned; family life broken up; cities emptied; property taken by Angkar"><rect x="0" y="0" width="480" height="300" fill="#ffffff"/><text x="240" y="20" font-size="13" font-family="sans-serif" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2937" font-weight="bold">Year Zero: a society turned upside down</text><rect x="20" y="32" width="200" height="28" fill="#bae6fd" stroke="#334155"/><text x="120" y="51" font-size="12" font-family="sans-serif" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2937" font-weight="bold">Cambodia before 1975</text><rect x="260" y="32" width="200" height="28" fill="#fecaca" stroke="#334155"/><text x="360" y="51" font-size="12" font-family="sans-serif" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f2937" font-weight="bold">Democratic Kampuchea</text><g font-size="11" font-family="sans-serif" fill="#1f2937"><rect x="20" y="66" width="200" height="30" fill="#f8fafc" stroke="#334155"/><text x="30" y="85">Money, markets, shops</text><rect x="260" y="66" width="200" height="30" fill="#fef2f2" stroke="#334155"/><text x="270" y="85">Money abolished, no markets</text><rect x="20" y="102" width="200" height="30" fill="#f8fafc" stroke="#334155"/><text x="30" y="121">Schools and universities</text><rect x="260" y="102" width="200" height="30" fill="#fef2f2" stroke="#334155"/><text x="270" y="121">Schools closed, books destroyed</text><rect x="20" y="138" width="200" height="30" fill="#f8fafc" stroke="#334155"/><text x="30" y="157">Buddhist temples and monks</text><rect x="260" y="138" width="200" height="30" fill="#fef2f2" stroke="#334155"/><text x="270" y="157">Religion banned</text><rect x="20" y="174" width="200" height="30" fill="#f8fafc" stroke="#334155"/><text x="30" y="193">Family homes and meals</text><rect x="260" y="174" width="200" height="30" fill="#fef2f2" stroke="#334155"/><text x="270" y="193">Families split, work units</text><rect x="20" y="210" width="200" height="30" fill="#f8fafc" stroke="#334155"/><text x="30" y="229">Busy towns and cities</text><rect x="260" y="210" width="200" height="30" fill="#fef2f2" stroke="#334155"/><text x="270" y="229">Cities emptied in days</text><rect x="20" y="246" width="200" height="30" fill="#f8fafc" stroke="#334155"/><text x="30" y="265">Own land, house, belongings</text><rect x="260" y="246" width="200" height="30" fill="#fef2f2" stroke="#334155"/><text x="270" y="265">Everything owned by Angkar</text></g><g stroke="#1f2937" stroke-width="2"><line x1="224" y1="81" x2="252" y2="81"/><line x1="224" y1="117" x2="252" y2="117"/><line x1="224" y1="153" x2="252" y2="153"/><line x1="224" y1="189" x2="252" y2="189"/><line x1="224" y1="225" x2="252" y2="225"/><line x1="224" y1="261" x2="252" y2="261"/></g><g fill="#1f2937"><polygon points="252,77 258,81 252,85"/><polygon points="252,113 258,117 252,121"/><polygon points="252,149 258,153 252,157"/><polygon points="252,185 258,189 252,193"/><polygon points="252,221 258,225 252,229"/><polygon points="252,257 258,261 252,265"/></g><text x="240" y="292" font-size="11" font-family="sans-serif" text-anchor="middle" fill="#334155">The aim: erase the past and make everyone serve Angkar alone.</text></svg>`,
