      whyItWorks:
        "Why did a regime kill so many of its own people? The Khmer Rouge's ideology divided people into 'pure' and 'enemy', and anyone linked to the old society, to foreign countries or to a minority group fell into the second category. Impossible targets failed, and the leaders explained failure as sabotage — so every failure produced more 'enemies'. Secrecy and isolation meant no one outside could stop it. Understanding this chain matters because the same warning signs — dividing people into groups, dehumanising language, rule without courts — have appeared before other mass atrocities.",
      strategies: ["Check the provenance (NOP)", "Cross-reference sources", "Judge significance"],
